\section{\code{score}}{

\verb{pagerankr score EDGES [--damping X] [--accepted-placements LIST] [--placement-weights LIST] [--out FILE]}

\code{EDGES} is a CSV file with a header row. It needs \code{from} and \code{to} columns
and may have a \code{placement} column (the region each link sits in) and a
numeric \code{weight} column; other columns are ignored. Scoring uses the
\code{\link[=pagerank]{pagerank()}} defaults: URLs are cleaned, self-loops and isolates are
dropped and duplicate edges collapse.
\itemize{
\item \verb{--damping X}: the damping factor, a number in \verb{[0, 1)}. Default \code{0.85}.
\code{\link[=pagerank]{pagerank()}} itself accepts \code{1}, but with no teleport the scores are not
well defined on most graphs (the solver can return \code{NaN}), so \code{score}
refuses it.
\item \verb{--accepted-placements LIST}: comma-separated placements to keep, from
\code{content}, \code{nav}, \code{header}, \code{footer} and \code{aside}. Edges placed elsewhere
are dropped. Needs a \code{placement} column.
\item \verb{--placement-weights LIST}: comma-separated \code{placement=weight} pairs, for
example \verb{content=1,nav=0.1,header=0.1,footer=0.1,aside=0.1}. Placements
not named keep weight 1. Needs a \code{placement} column and cannot be combined
with a \code{weight} column.
\item \verb{--out FILE}: write the CSV to \code{FILE} instead of standard output.
}

The output has one row per scored node, highest written PageRank first and
rows that print the same PageRank in \code{node_name} order:
\describe{
\item{\code{node_name}}{The node, after URL cleaning.}
\item{\code{pagerank}}{Its PageRank. The column sums to 1.}
\item{\code{cheirank}}{Its CheiRank, PageRank with \code{reverse = TRUE}. The column
sums to 1.}
}
}

\section{\code{compare}}{

\verb{pagerankr compare SCORES_A SCORES_B [--metric pagerank|cheirank] [--format csv|json] [--out FILE]}

\code{SCORES_A} (the baseline) and \code{SCORES_B} are CSV files with a \code{node_name}
column and the metric's column, as \code{score} writes them. Each \code{node_name}
appears once and no cell in either column is blank.
\itemize{
\item \code{--metric}: which score to compare, \code{pagerank} (default) or \code{cheirank}.
\item \code{--format}: \code{csv} (default) or \code{json}.
\item \verb{--out FILE}: write to \code{FILE} instead of standard output.
}

The CSV has the \code{\link[=compare_pagerank]{compare_pagerank()}} columns, with the metric's name in
place of \code{pagerank}: \code{node_name}, \verb{<metric>_a}, \verb{<metric>_b}, \code{delta},
\code{pct_change}, \code{rank_a}, \code{rank_b} and \code{rank_delta}, largest absolute change
first. A node missing from one file has empty cells for that side.

The JSON is one object: \code{metric} (the metric compared), \code{summary} (the
\code{\link[=compare_pagerank]{compare_pagerank()}} summary: \code{spearman_rho}, \code{mean_abs_delta},
\code{nodes_gained} and \code{nodes_lost}) and \code{nodes}, an array with one object per
row of the CSV. A missing value is \code{null}.
